LATENT DISPATCH
Wed Sep 23 · 5 items · ~4 min

SABLE-3.1 MINI
The point release comes to the small model: same refusal fixes, same long-context improvement, same price. Sable-3.1 Mini vs. Ferrite-70B is now the interesting mid-tier comparison; Mini is cheaper per token and slightly worse on extraction. Our head-to-head: https://latentdispatch.ai/p/mini-vs-ferrite

AGENT OBSERVABILITY: FIVE TOOLS COMPARED
We looked at Brindle, Larkspur's built-in replay, and three others on what they capture (every call? every tool result? cost per step?) and what they cost. Summary: the framework-native options are good enough for most teams; the standalone tools win when you run agents across several frameworks. https://latentdispatch.ai/p/observability-compared

Q3 OPEN-WEIGHTS SCORECARD
Ferrite-70B is the story of the quarter. Tanager-Coder 2 second. The 8B class got crowded and good. Full scorecard with the task-family breakdown: https://latentdispatch.ai/p/q3-scorecard

NIMBUS ADDS STAGED ROLLOUTS
As promised in the post-mortem. Region by region, with automatic rollback on error-rate increase.

SHORT TAKES
· Lodestone-R2 Mini gets prompt caching.
· A good thread on structuring prompts for cache hit rates: static first, dynamic last, no timestamps. https://latentdispatch.ai/p/cache-structure
· Quillon publishes Quillon-M's page-level accuracy by document type. Refreshing transparency.

